ed compatibility cases before a retain/update decision. | #700 coordinates; T18#665 unit harness, T2#649/T34#681/T35#682 standalone/accepted test contexts. Complete the consumer list from source; do not assume these examples exhaust it. |\n| rcheevos, inherited T20 R13 | Map top12.2.1 and nested10.7.0 markers to actual selected source files and upstream revision(s); assess applicability of dated [12.5.0](https://github.com/RetroAchievements/rcheevos/releases/tag/v12.5.0) changes. | T20#667 retains excluded hash/RVZ deltas and three-hasher obligations. This tracks the inherited provenance question, not a duplicate bug or a new defect finding. |\n| libretro-common | Identify upstream revision(s) for the mixed imported subset and delimit local differences before a carry/update recommendation. | #700 with T20#667; excluded MD5/hash behavior stays T20. |\n| NanoSVG | Map the imported snapshot and later optimization delta to upstream commits, preserving origin/notices and rendering-owner boundaries. | #700 with T36#683; no visual correctness or removal conclusion from metadata. |\n| id3v2lib | Resolve flattened-import/current upstream revision, distinguish CMake2.0 from historical README1.0 wording, and account for selected library/vendor-test targets. | #700 with T37#684 configuration context; no test/asset removal inferred. |\n\nPugixml's selected system recipe and uninitialized gitlink remain distinct; no submodule fetch is implied. #256 keeps its upstream submission map, #585 its unsent contribution queue, and existing behavioral bugs retain their owners.\n\n## Execution boundary\n\nThe coordinator chooses an immutable integration baseline, worktree, exact upstream inputs and acquisition method, narrowly affected paths, tests and review depth before dispatch. No source acquisition, update, compiler, guest, external reviewer, physical/cloud action or permission response is authorized by creating this backlog issue.\n\nAlready written: vendor code, notices and accepted test receipts pre-exist; keep original identities/allowed failures intact. Can this be done on the VM? Provenance comparison uses immutable source records. Any later changed-library behavior is verified with the owning pure tests and GENERIC_X64 where required under a separate capacity/runtime dispatch. No physical-device fact is needed for this planning task.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A retained five-group provenance matrix binds the selected local tree, exact upstream revision(s), file-level comparison and actual build consumers; it resolves each named mapping question or identifies an externally unavailable input with an explicit coordinator disposition and owned follow-up.\n- [ ] A doctest evaluation packet binds old/new header identities, complete affected consumer/compiler configuration, meaningful failing controls and fresh compatibility results. Existing allowed failures remain classified; historical receipts are not relabeled as fresh tests.\n- [ ] A dated coordinator decision for each group states retain/update, rationale and revisit trigger. R13 remains explicitly linked to T20's excluded hash/RVZ obligations; no duplicate closure or ownership transfer.\n- [ ] Any selected update lands through its own scoped implementation/verification brief with source/notices preserved and required pure-test/guest evidence; a retain outcome names its bounded reason without claiming an unmeasured compatibility result.\n- [ ] #691/#700 reference the final matrix and decisions; the milestone queue reflects the actual disposition. No dependency removal or upstream submission follows automatically.\n\nTrace: T44 #1413 PL-001 and #691 questions1–3; immutable stage-b/vendor-groups.json and05/07 at923dd5371ded83fa965449a83f7a16d15a174772; inherited T20 R13 in accepted780827c5ba40fc78a741f2f9e0655872c8d8d0c7:02-forward-audit.md lines169–189.",
